    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Dairy proteins, eggs and meat consistently score highest on the modern DIAAS scale, with soy close behind, pea and rice lower on their own, and collagen at zero. But quality is a per-gram measure. Once you eat enough total protein from a reasonable mix of sources, the gap between a top-scoring and a middling protein shrinks to something most people will never notice. Here is what the scores mean, where they come from, and when they should change what you buy.
        </p>

        <div id="what-quality-means" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What Protein Quality Actually Measures
          </h2>

          <p>
            Your body breaks every protein you eat down into amino acids, then rebuilds them into muscle, enzymes, hormones, skin and everything else. Twenty amino acids are used to build human protein. Nine of them are essential, meaning your body cannot make them and they have to come from food: histidine, isoleucine, leucine, lysine, methionine, phenylalanine, threonine, tryptophan and valine.
          </p>

          <p>
            Protein quality is a measure of how well a single protein covers those nine, gram for gram. It has two parts. The first is composition: does the protein contain each essential amino acid in roughly the proportions the human body needs? The second is digestibility: of the amino acids on paper, how many actually make it from your gut into your bloodstream?
          </p>

          <p>
            A protein can fail on either count. Wheat protein is reasonably digestible but short on lysine. Some raw legumes have a decent amino acid profile but contain compounds such as trypsin inhibitors and phytates that reduce how much you absorb. Collagen is highly digestible but is missing tryptophan entirely. The score you see quoted for a protein is an attempt to fold both factors into one number.
          </p>

          <p>
            The key idea behind every scoring system is the limiting amino acid. If a protein supplies 100 percent of your need for eight essential amino acids but only 60 percent of your need for lysine, your body can only use it as if it were 60 percent adequate for building new protein. The scarcest essential amino acid sets the ceiling, much like the shortest stave in a barrel sets how much water it holds.
          </p>

          <p>
            That is why protein quality matters less for a mixed diet than for a single food. Beans are low in methionine and grains are low in lysine, but eaten across the same day they cover each other. Quality scores are most useful when one protein source supplies a large share of your intake, which is exactly the situation with a daily protein shake.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="pdcaas-vs-diaas" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. PDCAAS vs DIAAS: Two Ways to Score a Protein
          </h2>

          <p>
            The older system is PDCAAS, the Protein Digestibility Corrected Amino Acid Score, adopted by the FAO and WHO in the early 1990s. It compares a protein's limiting amino acid against a reference pattern of human requirements, then multiplies by a single digestibility figure for the whole protein, traditionally measured in rats from what is left in the feces. Scores are capped at 1.0.
          </p>

          <p>
            PDCAAS is still the system US food labels use. Under FDA rules, if a product lists a percent Daily Value for protein, that figure has to be corrected for PDCAAS. So a powder with 20 grams of a lower-scoring protein can legitimately show a smaller percent Daily Value than one with 20 grams of whey, even though the grams on the label are identical.
          </p>

          <p>
            PDCAAS has two well-known flaws. The cap at 1.0 hides real differences: whey, casein, egg and soy isolate all score 1.0, even though some of them exceed human requirements by a wide margin. And fecal digestibility overestimates what you absorb, because bacteria in the large intestine consume amino acids that were never absorbed in the small intestine, where absorption actually happens.
          </p>

          <p>
            In 2013 an FAO expert consultation recommended a replacement, the Digestible Indispensable Amino Acid Score, or DIAAS. It measures digestibility of each essential amino acid individually at the end of the small intestine, usually in growing pigs, which digest protein more like people than rats do. It also stops capping the score, so a protein that oversupplies essential amino acids can score above 100.
          </p>

          <p>
            The FAO proposed three bands for DIAAS. A score of 100 or more qualifies as an excellent or high-quality protein. A score from 75 to 99 qualifies as a good protein. Below 75, no protein quality claim should be made. Our <a href="/whey-vs-plant-protein/">whey vs plant protein</a> comparison goes deeper into how those bands play out for dairy versus plant powders specifically.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
