            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="score-table" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. DIAAS and PDCAAS Scores for Common Foods and Powders
          </h2>

          <p>
            Below are scores reported in the research literature for proteins you are likely to eat or buy as powder. Treat every figure as approximate. DIAAS values change with the age reference pattern used, the specific ingredient batch, and whether the food was tested raw or cooked, and different labs report different numbers for the same protein. We have shown ranges where published values disagree.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Protein</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">DIAAS (approx.)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">PDCAAS</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Limiting amino acid</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Whole milk powder</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 1.16</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1.0</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">None</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Milk protein concentrate</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Above 1.0</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1.0</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">None</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Whey protein isolate</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 1.00-1.09</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1.0</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">None</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Casein</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Above 1.0</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1.0</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">None</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Whole egg, boiled</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 1.13</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1.0</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">None</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Beef</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 1.12</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 0.92</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">None at DIAAS level</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Chicken breast</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 1.08</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Not widely reported</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">None</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Soy protein isolate</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 0.84-0.90</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 0.9-1.0</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Sulfur amino acids</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Pea protein concentrate</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 0.62-0.82</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 0.75-0.89</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Methionine and cysteine</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Rice protein concentrate</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 0.37-0.47</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 0.42-0.50</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Lysine</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Wheat / wheat gluten</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Below 0.75 (wheat)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 0.25 (gluten)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Lysine</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Collagen peptides</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">0</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">0</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Tryptophan (absent)</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The dairy figures come largely from pig studies at the University of Illinois. Mathai, Liu and Stein, writing in the British Journal of Nutrition in 2017, found that whey protein isolate, whey protein concentrate and milk protein concentrate all reached the excellent band for people over six months of age, while soy protein isolate landed in the good band and pea protein concentrate fell below 75. Earlier work by Rutherfurd and colleagues reported a DIAAS of about 1.09 for whey isolate, 0.90 for soy isolate, 0.82 for pea concentrate and 0.37 for rice concentrate.
          </p>

          <p>
            Not every review agrees on the order at the top. A 2020 overview by Herreman and colleagues in Food Science and Nutrition pooled DIAAS data from many studies and placed casein, egg, pork and potato protein in the excellent tier, with whey and soy one tier down in the high-quality band of 75 and above. Pea, rice, hemp, oat and gelatin fell into the no-claim category. The shuffling at the top reflects study methods more than any meaningful difference between dairy proteins; all of them are excellent sources.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two patterns hold across every dataset. Animal proteins cluster at or above 100, and single plant proteins score lower, with soy the clear plant leader. And processing matters: isolates and concentrates usually score better than the whole plant food they came from, because processing strips out fiber and much of the compounds that block digestion. Cooked peas score well below pea protein isolate, for example.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">READING THE TABLE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">A score below 1.0 does not mean the protein is useless</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Pea protein at a DIAAS of roughly 0.8 still delivers most of its essential amino acids. In practice it means you need a little more of it, or a complementary protein alongside it, to match what a dairy protein delivers gram for gram.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="complete-protein" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Complete vs Incomplete Protein (and Why Collagen Scores Zero)
          </h2>

          <p>
            A complete protein is usually defined as one that contains all nine essential amino acids in useful amounts. By that definition dairy, eggs, meat, fish and soy are complete, and so are pea and rice protein, technically, because they contain all nine. The catch is that pea is relatively low in methionine and rice is relatively low in lysine, which is why their scores lag.
          </p>

          <p>
            That is the logic behind pea and rice blends. Pea protein is rich in lysine but short on sulfur amino acids. Rice protein is the reverse. Combined, typically in ratios somewhere around 70 percent pea to 30 percent rice, each covers the other's gap, and the blend's amino acid profile starts to resemble dairy. Herreman's review noted that soy, pea and potato proteins complement a broad range of other plant proteins when mixed at the right ratios.
          </p>

          <p>
            Collagen is the exception that is genuinely incomplete. It contains no tryptophan at all, very little leucine, and limited methionine, while being loaded with glycine, proline and hydroxyproline. That gives it a PDCAAS and DIAAS of zero. It is not a poor complete protein; it is missing an essential amino acid, so it cannot support muscle protein building on its own.
          </p>

          <p>
            The research reflects this. In a 2020 trial in the American Journal of Clinical Nutrition, Oikawa and colleagues gave healthy older women 30 grams of whey or collagen twice a day. Whey raised muscle protein synthesis, with and without resistance exercise; collagen did not. If you buy collagen for skin, joints or tendons, count it as a supplement in its own right, not as a substitute for your daily protein. Our <a href="/best-collagen-protein-powder/">collagen protein powder guide</a> covers when it makes sense.
          </p>

          <p>
            Watch for one labeling trick. Some powders blend collagen with whey to inflate the grams of protein on the front of the tub while using less of the expensive ingredient. If collagen, gelatin or hydrolyzed beef appears high on the ingredient list of a powder sold for muscle, the effective quality is lower than the protein number suggests.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="leucine" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Leucine: The Number Quality Scores Don't Show
          </h2>

          <p>
            Quality scores tell you whether a protein meets basic human requirements. They do not tell you how strongly it switches on muscle building after a meal. That job falls largely to leucine, an essential amino acid that acts as a signal to start muscle protein synthesis, in addition to being a building block.
          </p>

          <p>
            Research from McMaster University and elsewhere suggests that a meal needs to deliver roughly 2 to 3 grams of leucine to maximally stimulate muscle protein synthesis in young adults, and somewhat more in older adults. About 25 grams of whey provides around 3 grams of leucine, which is one reason whey performs so well in short-term muscle studies. Our guide to <a href="/muscle-protein-synthesis-supplements/">muscle protein synthesis supplements</a> covers the leucine threshold in more detail.
          </p>

          <p>
            Leucine content varies more than most people expect. Whey typically runs around 11 to 12 percent leucine. A 2018 analysis by Gorissen and colleagues in Amino Acids measured 9.0 percent in milk protein, 7.0 percent in egg protein, and 7.6 percent in human muscle itself. Many plant isolates such as pea and soy sit around 8 percent on typical ingredient specifications, while hemp measured as low as 5.1 percent. Collagen is roughly 3 percent.
          </p>

          <p>
            The same study also measured total essential amino acid content, which tracks quality closely. Whey came in at 43 percent essential amino acids, milk at 39 percent, casein at 34 percent and egg at 32 percent. Oat, lupin and wheat protein isolates came in at 21 to 22 percent. A plant protein with fewer essential amino acids and less leucine per gram simply needs a bigger serving to hit the same targets.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Leucine and EAA Content</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">43%</p>
