              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">0</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Tryptophan in collagen</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="when-it-matters" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. When Protein Quality Matters and When Total Intake Wins
          </h2>

          <p>
            Here is the honest bottom line most quality charts leave out: for a healthy adult eating enough protein from varied sources, the quality of any single protein makes very little difference to results. Total daily intake is the variable that moves muscle, satiety and recovery. Our guide on <a href="/how-much-protein-do-i-need/">how much protein you need</a> sets out the targets, and the 2018 Morton meta-analysis put the point of diminishing returns for lifters around 1.6 grams per kilogram per day.
          </p>

          <p>
            Long-term training studies back this up. In a 2021 study in Sports Medicine, Hevia-Larraín and colleagues compared young vegan and omnivorous men on a 12-week resistance program, with protein intake kept high in both groups. Muscle and strength gains were not different. A 2021 meta-analysis in Nutrients by Lim and colleagues found no overall difference in absolute lean mass or strength between animal and plant protein, though animal protein produced a small lean-mass advantage, about 0.4 kilograms, in adults under 50.
          </p>

          <p>
            Quality starts to matter more in a few specific situations. The first is when total protein is low or borderline, because then every gram has to work harder. The second is older age: older muscle responds less to a given dose of protein, so a leucine-rich, highly digestible protein makes it easier to cross the threshold without eating huge portions. The third is when one protein supplies most of your intake, such as in a restrictive diet or heavy reliance on shakes.
          </p>

          <p>
            Calorie-restricted diets are a fourth case. If you are dieting hard or using a GLP-1 medication and appetite is low, a higher-quality protein delivers more usable amino acids per calorie and per gram you can actually manage to eat. Our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-glp-1/">protein powder for GLP-1 users</a> guide covers that situation, and anyone on medication should plan intake with their doctor or a registered dietitian.
          </p>

          <p>
            For everyone else, the practical rule is simple: hit your daily total, spread it across three or four meals, and if you lean on plant proteins, eat a little more or mix sources. Quality is a tiebreaker, not the main event.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="buying" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. How to Use Protein Quality When Buying a Powder
          </h2>

          <p>
            If you tolerate dairy, whey and milk proteins give you the highest quality per scoop, and the differences among them are about digestion speed and lactose rather than amino acids. Whey concentrate is usually the cheapest per gram of high-quality protein; isolate costs more and suits people sensitive to lactose. Our <a href="/types-of-protein-powder/">guide to every type of protein powder</a> walks through those trade-offs by goal.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you avoid dairy, soy isolate is the highest-scoring single plant protein and has the most research behind it for muscle. Pea-based powders work well, especially blended with rice or another complementary protein; aim for a serving that delivers at least 25 to 30 grams of protein to compensate for the lower leucine. Egg white protein is a strong dairy-free option for people who eat animal products.
          </p>

          <p>
            Read the ingredient list, not just the protein number. The protein source listed first makes up the largest share. Be skeptical of proprietary blends that do not state amounts, of added collagen or gelatin in muscle-focused products, and of added free amino acids such as glycine or taurine, which can inflate the protein figure on the label, a practice known as amino spiking.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, quality on paper is only useful if the product contains what the label claims. Third-party certification such as NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Choice verifies label accuracy and screens for banned substances, which matters more for most buyers than a few DIAAS points. Our <a href="/is-protein-powder-safe/">protein powder safety guide</a> explains what those programs check, and our <a href="/best-protein-powder/">best protein powder</a> rankings favor tested products.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating PDCAAS 1.0 as a tie</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Whey, casein, egg and soy isolate all score 1.0 on PDCAAS only because the score is capped. DIAAS shows dairy and egg above 100 and soy somewhat lower. The difference is modest, but if you are comparing proteins closely, DIAAS gives you the more accurate picture.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Counting collagen toward your muscle protein</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Collagen has a quality score of zero because it lacks tryptophan and is very low in leucine. It may have its own uses for skin and connective tissue, but grams of collagen should not count toward a daily protein target meant to support muscle.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Writing off plant protein entirely</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A lower score means you need slightly more of a plant protein or a complementary blend, not that it cannot build muscle. Training studies with adequate total protein show vegan and omnivorous lifters gaining similarly. Dose and total intake close most of the gap.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Obsessing over quality while undereating</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Switching from pea to whey will not fix a diet that delivers 60 grams of protein a day when you need 120. Total daily intake explains far more of your results than the score of any single source. Fix the total first, then refine the sources.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting the front label protein number blindly</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Protein grams on the front can be inflated with collagen, gelatin or free amino acids. Check the ingredient order, look for a published amino acid profile, and favor products with third-party certification that verifies the label matches what is in the tub.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the highest quality protein?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On the DIAAS scale, dairy proteins such as milk, casein and whey, along with eggs and meats like beef and pork, typically score at or above 100, the top band. Among protein powders, whey, casein and milk protein blends deliver the highest quality per scoop. Soy isolate is the highest-scoring plant protein, usually in the good band of 75 to 99.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between PDCAAS and DIAAS?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                PDCAAS uses a single fecal digestibility figure for the whole protein and caps scores at 1.0. DIAAS measures the digestibility of each essential amino acid at the end of the small intestine and does not cap scores, so it separates top proteins more accurately. The FAO recommended DIAAS in 2013, but US labels still use PDCAAS.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is whey or egg protein higher quality?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Both sit in the excellent band. Reported DIAAS values are around 1.0 to 1.09 for whey isolate and about 1.13 for boiled whole egg, and some reviews rank egg slightly higher. Whey contains more leucine per gram, which gives it an edge for stimulating muscle protein synthesis in short-term studies. In practice either is an excellent choice.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is pea protein a complete protein?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Technically yes, because it contains all nine essential amino acids. But it is relatively low in methionine and cysteine, so its DIAAS usually falls below the 75 mark when tested alone. Blending pea with rice protein, which is low in lysine but higher in sulfur amino acids, produces a profile much closer to dairy.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does protein quality matter if I eat enough protein?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Much less than most people think. When total protein intake is adequate and comes from varied sources, training studies find similar muscle and strength gains across plant and animal diets. Quality matters more when intake is low, appetite is limited, you are older, or a single protein supplies most of your daily total.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why does collagen have a protein quality score of zero?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Collagen contains no tryptophan, one of the nine essential amino acids. Because quality scores are set by the most limiting essential amino acid, a protein completely missing one scores zero. Collagen is also low in leucine. That makes it unsuitable as a main protein source for muscle, though it may still be useful for other purposes.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do protein powder labels show quality scores?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Rarely directly. In the US, if a label lists a percent Daily Value for protein, that figure must be corrected by PDCAAS, so a lower-quality protein will show a lower percent Daily Value for the same grams. Some brands publish an amino acid profile, which lets you check leucine and essential amino acid content yourself.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

